<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<ecoSpold xmlns="http://www.EcoInvent.org/EcoSpold02">
  <activityDataset>
    <activityDescription>
      <activity id="90fe0100-329f-5fea-81e8-4625d5674935" activityNameId="c0c3bb56-eb79-58cc-a965-37c75c66b1ae" activityNameContextId="f44c43c1-5cc5-54a6-ade6-24435bd12c83" type="1" specialActivityType="0" energyValues="0">
        <activityName xml:lang="en">Desktop computer, without screen, at plant</activityName>
        <generalComment>
          <text xml:lang="en" index="1">Full activity name: Desktop computer, without screen, at plant
This dataset can be applied to describe the production (without monitor!) of a common desktop computer in the last 3 years in the reference year 2010 - 2015 (Intel® Core™ i5-8500, 6 Cores/9MB/6T/up to 4.1GH, 500 GB RAM HDD, 8 GB working memory, total weight without screen and cardboard packaging 8.8 kg). The information is based on own measurements of computer parts at Empa Laboratories. Computer parts like Graphic card, Network card, Hard disk drive, CD Rom drive, Power supply unit, Printed wiring boards (e.g. motherboard) and batteries are inventoried in individual ecoinvent datasets. In 2023, this dataset has been updated to reflect the recent laptops found in the literature (Babbit et al 2020; UUID: f9531744-2e9e-3481-ba7e-5aae28c6b126
Describes the production of a desktop computer without screen. Calculated per 1 produced computer (1 unit). Included are the materials (mainly metals and plastics) with their respective manufacturing processes (e.g. sheet rolling or blow moulding). Further inventoried is the infrastructure (factory), the electricity for the assembly of the desktop computer, the water consumption and industrial waste water, the required ship, rail and road transport for input materials, the packaging, plus the disposal of the computer.
Location: The data is based on weighing a typical desktop computer at the EMPA laboratories in St. Gallen + supplemented with new data from recent literature. Such a desktop computer like other PC brands may be assembled anywhere in the world.
Technology: The production of a desktop computer includes the steel processing steps rolling, cutting, mill cutting, and coating. Plastic parts are blow moulded or extruded into required shapes. The process technology of the parts (capacitors, resistors, microchips etc.) mounted onto the printed wiring board are described in the individual datasets.
Time period: Unspecified
Production volume: unknown
Sampling: Own measurements at laboratory (weighing) and manufacturer information.
Extrapolations: see Geography and Technology
Uncertainty adjustments: see Geography and Technology
BAFU mapped export: unsupported biosphere exchanges excluded; see audit/. Local UUIDs and generic activity classification are export conventions. Source: process_f9531744-2e9e-3481-ba7e-5aae28c6b126.xml</text>
        </generalComment>
      </activity>
      <geography geographyId="f4808887-5a57-5892-975e-ba0565a93e23" geographyContextId="f44c43c1-5cc5-54a6-ade6-24435bd12c83">
